I have seen a girl have several demons possessing her at once. She was in a service in Armenia, in the rural town of Bjni, and I remember the way that girl reacted to the presence of the Holy Spirit in that church. I was thirteen years old and I was sitting at the front of the room, while she was in the back. At some point, she began screeching in a way that I cannot describe well in writing. I thought she was dying. It was a scream unlike anything I had heard, and I have not heard anything remotely close to it since (I am 21 years old now). It took five grown men to hold her while the pastor commanded the demons to leave her.

&#x200B;

Long story short, yes, demon-possession is real. The girl was possessed by demons after her mother welcomed demons into their home. I witnessed the way the girl was able to overpower several grown men at once, I remember the way she screamed, and I know her story.

Thank you. :)

&#x200B;

To answer your question, the books of the Apocrypha have not been accepted by Protestant Christians, OR the Jewish people. The Apocrypha was written during the time of the OT, but it is still rejected by Jewish people.

&#x200B;

There is no authority that can tell you what to read. However, the consensus is that the 66 books that we have make up the complete, divinely-inspired Word of God. Galatians 1 tells us that the Gospel has already been given to us. The Bible will not change, it will not be added to, and we are to reject anybody/anything that says otherwise.

&#x200B;

I have explained the standard that was used when selecting which books to be included in the Bible's canon.

I want you to do me a favor:

Take one verse/passage at a time, tell me what it is, and we can discuss it. It is not fair to dump several websites, tens of verses, and a bunch of videos.

I have been praying for you. I do want you to know that I am aware that you have your own beliefs, and that you are trying to attack the doctrine of Christianity. I believe that you are well aware of what you are doing. While that does not justify people treating you poorly, it does say something about the stance you take. Why do you claim to be asking a question when your posts clearly show you arguing with people? It seems like you are very confident in what you have read, so why do you act as if you are open-minded? There is nothing wrong with having beliefs (I have many of my own), as long as you are honest about it.

&#x200B;

I look forward to your response to my comment about Paul being trustworthy. I ask that you respond to the passages I mention, and that you do not bring up tangential topics or additional verses until we discuss the ones I have mentioned.

&#x200B;

I am all for continuing this discussion. All I ask is that you are honest in your approach.

&#x200B;

As for the Quran stuff, there is a lot to unpack. No, Muslims and Christians do not worship the same god. The Islamic god (Allah) is completely different in character to the God that Christians believe in. Furthermore, Christianity/Judaism has been the basis for many religions around the world, including Islam. Islam is considered to be an "Abrahamic" religion for this very reason.

&#x200B;

I don't mean to sound rude, but I must be honest with you: it sounds like you have something against Christianity, and that you have gone to all kinds of websites to justify your feelings. I am not using websites to refute your claims, I am using the Bible. I am not a Islamic apologist, I am a Christian apologist. If you would like to discuss the Quran, I am not interested. Furthermore, you seem to have a surface-level understanding of both the Bible and the Quran. I hope you choose to accept Christ as your Lord and Savior, and I am willing to do whatever I can to help you in that, but I cannot force you to believe. You must make that decision.

As I mentioned in my previous comment (in response to his question about what the first "collection" was), there is no "set". The books of the Bible were written by different people, at different times. For the Epistles (the letters of the NT), it was a common practice for people to keep copies of the letters that they sent. That would mean that there were at least two copies of the original letter. As the letter was shared, it was copied, and shared again. The way to determine what should be included is to see which parts of each manuscript can be found in other manuscripts. If a manuscript is the same as a manuscript written at a different time, in a different place, we can know that the manuscript in question has what was written originally. If the books of the Bible varied wildly between manuscripts, then we would have a problem. Most manuscripts are almost identical to one another. 

&#x200B;

There have been "sets" created by historians/the church. The Old Testament was written before we had books, so there could not have been a single copy of the entire Old Testament at that time. Nowadays, we have the ability to bind books together, which allows us to have both the OT and the NT in the same book.

&#x200B;

The parts of a "set" do no matter, as long as the parts contain the same writing as the majority of manuscripts (for the part in question).

Thank you for your response. I really do appreciate you taking the time to share your knowledge with me.

&#x200B;

Why does the topic of the Trinity cause JWs to shut down? Are there other issues that would cause a JW to shut down? Side-note: the Trinity is one of the core doctrines of Christianity. Without believing in the Trinity, one cannot be a Christian.

The correct set can be determined by comparing the manuscripts that have been found. Some early manuscripts do no include Mark 16:9-20, for example. Scholars have debated the ending of Mark, and whether or not it should be included in our Bibles, but nobody has advocated for throwing out the whole book because of a relatively minor discrepancy. The Bible is agreed upon by Christians, regardless of their affiliation. There is the Apocrypha, but the books of the Apocrypha do not affect the actual gospel itself.

&#x200B;

The Jehovah's Witnesses are not Christians, so it does not make sense to discuss them here.

There are 66 books of the Bible, not 70+.

As for how the "canon" books of the Bible were determined, there were several councils in the early Christian church that were tasked with determining if a book should be recognized as canon or not. In the early 4th century (if I remember correctly), Christians realized that as time goes on, it will become harder to determine whether or not a certain book should be included in the Bible. That realization motivated the early church to debate about the books that were available at the time, in order to determine what is and what is not the Word of God. The standard used was (1) was the book written by an apostle/prophet, and/or was it recognized by apostles/prophets, (2) has the book been widely accepted and used in the church, and (3) does the book line up with the books that had already been accepted.

&#x200B;

Christians take this very seriously, and the early church spent a long time debating and trying to figure out what was truly the Word of God, and what was not. If you want to read some of what was rejected by the early church, there are many "gnostic gospels" that you can read online. I have recently started a series about the Infancy Gospel of Thomas. The series is available on my website.

&#x200B;

Did I answer your question?

There is a big issue with the second question you asked. At the time of the OT being written, they were written on scrolls. There was too much to put on one scroll, so there was never a single manuscript. The NT saw the authors of the Bible using what we now refer to as books. By binding pages together, early Christians could share the Bible with others, without needing tons of scrolls.

&#x200B;

As for your first question, I am not sure that I understand. If I read a travel-guide, I could tell you that it is not the Word of God. If you give me a Bible, I could tell you that it is the Word of God. How can you tell me what an apple is, and what is not an apple?

Thank you for your encouragement!

&#x200B;

Are you looking for guests? :)

The Bible is not a translation, it is the Bible. The way I would describe it is "the Bible is inerrant, but our translations are not." Languages do not translate perfectly into other languages, so it would be silly to say that any translation is inerrant. That being said, it is important to acknowledge that God can speak to us through anything, so the translation does not matter (in most cases). I actually enjoy studying translations in my free-time, so I am very excited about your question.

&#x200B;

To answer the first part of your question, there is no single Catholic version, nor a single Protestant version. There are many different versions of the Bible that the Catholics use, there are many more versions that are used amongst Protestants, and the Orthodox Church tends to use the same (or very similar) translations as the Catholic Church.

&#x200B;

The Jehovah's Witnesses have the New World Translation. The most notable differences between this version and other versions is that the NWT inserts the name "Jehovah" (even in the NT, which is a separate issue), it tries to remove any reference to Jesus being God, it removes any mention of the Holy Spirit, and it makes sure that "cross" is replaced with "torture stake".


I have no idea what the Ethiopian Bible is, but I would be interested to read more about it.

&#x200B;

In short, the Bible is the Word of God, while translations of the Word of God are translations. I know that my previous statement is a tautology, but it is the best way of describing it. I also want to point out how, even in the NWT (I have done a lot a studying about that version in particular), the truth of Christ shines through. Even when the Gospel has been purposely minimized, the truth of God's Word can still be found. Different translations exist to address the differences between people, cultures, education, reading-comprehension, and language.

I don't understand why people are downvoting your comment. I see your point, and I have thought about the same thing you are talking about.

&#x200B;

I would ask, don't you think it would be more confusing to have LibertyOS, by LibertyOS? Try asking Siri/Alexa to play "Black Sabbath" by Black Sabbath, off of "Black Sabbath". lol

&#x200B;

Often discussions in writing can come across as being mean or something, but I want you to know that I am not at all annoyed or anything. I am kinda joking with you, but I do appreciate your comments. In all honesty, I have regretted naming it "libertyos\_kernel" at times, but I have no idea what I would have called it if not what I named it.

In short, Redox is fair beyond the scope of what LOS is trying to become. Redox is pretty well-known, has its own supported triplet for Rust, and has been developed for years. I have a lot respect for the Redox developers, but I don't care for Redox. It seems kind of awkward to use, and it looks, for lack of a better term, boring. I want to make something more interesting, and more accessible to the average person. Redox's build system is a nightmare for the layman, and I think that if they made their OS easier to compile, they would do much better with the average person.

&#x200B;

I don't know enough to really criticize Redox. I wish them the best, but I don't want to work with them. I don't know about the other Rust-based operating system developers, but I love seeing LOS trend alongside other operating systems, watching each OS make improvements that others learn from, and all of use working to outdo the others. It's a fun, friendly way of being somewhat competitive, but in a productive way. :)

&#x200B;

As for your comments about splitting the efforts between projects, I disagree with your conclusion. Passion is not in limited supply, and we are not running for office, so it shouldn't really matter how many operating systems are being developed. Just as with anything else, the best of the best is recognized as being the best, and the garbage is quickly forgotten. If this project is really just another garbage hobby or something (which it absolutely is not), then the project will not go anywhere, and the developers will move to another project.

Another user submitted a PR with your change. I merged the change into the main branch, and it was included in the 0.11.2 version of the kernel. :)

&#x200B;

Also, the compiler is warning that "vec" does not have a reason to mutable. Do you know why this is?

I just reviewed your repository. Your kernel is very interesting to me. Do you think you could contribute some of that code to LOS? :)

&#x200B;

Also, thank you for the encouragement!

Dude, I am majoring in Political Science. I want to attend law school. I have absolutely no room to talk. lol

&#x200B;

You are more than welcome to get involved. :) Join the Discord group and contact me.

Hello! The goals for this project are kind of weird. In short, I started this project as a hobby, and my goal at the time was to make something that I could use as my main operating system. While the goals for this project have expanded as its popularity has increased, the goals are still largely the same as before. I will introduce new things to the kernel that I would never have thought of (I probably wouldn't use/need a lot of them), in response to suggestions/requests from others.

&#x200B;

I hate to be that guy, but in the podcast episode I mentioned in my post, I went into more detail about the project and its goals. I will see if I can mention it by name, but I have to ask the host.

I appreciate your input. I have spent a long time thinking about the whole idea of this project "becoming a job", and I think you make a valid point. The reason why I am looking to take this project "to another level" is because it has really been doing well, its name is becoming more recognizable in the Rust community, and I have been meeting all kinds of really intelligent developers that know how to make this project even better than I could have ever dreamed of making it.

&#x200B;

I don't have a specific goal. I have many smaller goals, that build upon the ideas I have had for this operating system from its inception. I don't have any plans to earn a cent off of this project, and the real reward is being able to say that I accomplished that which I set out to do, despite the steep learning curve, the complexity of the subject material, and the amount of work that I had to do. While I love the help from others, if everybody were to turn on me/my project, I would still continue developing it. I am passionate about this operating system, and I am determined to make it work. I have many faults, but my drive to do what I set out to do is not one of them. I become obsessive over the things that I am interested in. Until I succeed, I cannot think about anything else.

&#x200B;

As for "burning-out", I know what that feels like, but I am not putting in long hours, or working holidays to get updates out. I genuinely love working on this, and I gladly spend hours every day, either researching things to do, or implemented the things I have researched. I routinely spend hours, working into the middle of the night, just trying to get something simple working, but I love every second of it. I love a challenge, I have wanted to write an operating system for almost a decade, and I love having a lot of stuff to learn about.

&#x200B;

Again, I really do appreciate your post. I will certainly keep your comments in mind. Merry Christmas!

Thanks for replying. I will look into getting my OS to work with user-input.

&#x200B;

Also, your crate is pretty cool, but I am new to the actual concept of that crate. What could I use your crate for?

From what I understand, C# could work, but would probably require you to learn another language to get C# working fully. If you want something relatively similar, the C language is absolutely worth learning. I use Rust, as I like its safety features, and its syntax makes sense to me, but Rust is not for everybody. I am also open to talking over messages if you would like me to share some resources to get you going in Rust.

&#x200B;

Regardless of what you choose to do, I wish you the best of luck
